{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III held the title of Count of Tripoli during the twelfth century and served as a major figure in the Crusader states. He was born into a powerful noble family that controlled significant lands along the Syrian coast. His father was Raymond II who ruled the County until he was assassinated by the Hashashin sect. This tragic event forced the young Raymond to assume leadership while still a minor which caused political instability within the realm. The regency was managed by his mother Hodierna of Jerusalem who acted as the guardian until he reached adulthood. Raymond eventually took control of his domains and became one of the most influential princes in the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nThe young count faced immediate challenges upon inheriting his title because the region was surrounded by hostile Muslim powers. He had to navigate complex diplomatic relationships with neighboring states while trying to secure his borders from constant threats. His youth made him vulnerable to manipulation by more experienced nobles within the kingdom of Jerusalem. The situation required careful management to prevent internal strife or external invasion during this critical period of his rule.\n\n*Political Alliances and Marriages*\nRaymond sought to strengthen his position through strategic marriages and alliances with other powerful families in the region. He married Eschiva of Bures which connected him to the lordship of Galilee and expanded his influence over central territories. These unions helped him build a network of support that proved vital during times of war and political maneuvering. His ability to maintain good relations with King Baldwin IV was particularly important given the king's illness and the resulting power struggles.\n\n*Role in the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nAs the kingdom faced increasing pressure from Saladin Raymond played a central role in the governance and defense of the realm. He served as regent for the young king and later acted as a key advisor during the crisis that led to the Battle of Hattin. His cautious approach to military engagements often put him at odds with more aggressive leaders like Guy of Lusignan. Raymond warned against rash actions that could lead to disaster but his advice was ultimately ignored by the faction supporting Guy. The defeat at Hattin resulted in the loss of Jerusalem and the collapse of much of the Crusader state. Raymond survived the battle and continued to defend Tripoli until his death in 1187. His legacy remains that of a pragmatic leader who tried to preserve the Crusader states through diplomacy and careful planning despite overwhelming odds.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0027734917}, {"token": " held", "logprob": -4.0018106}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0011433937}, {"token": " title", "logprob": -0.049986497}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00078649446}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.760826e-05}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -9.179157e-06}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.8887172}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.60793793}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.027838351}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -3.576285e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -4.768383e-06}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.042628054}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.16584088}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.00018890496}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.06418121}, {"token": " major", "logprob": -0.669133}, {"token": " figure", "logprob": -0.39210585}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.3663915}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00046812522}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.39384294}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -2.3841887e-06}, {"token": " states", "logprob": -0.09364723}, {"toke